      "exp_en": "For a patient under 18 years old, emancipation is a legal process where a minor petitions the court by themselves to be declared a legal adult. Emancipation laws vary from state to state. Emancipation ends the parental and legal duty to support the minor. It also ends the right of parents to make health decisions for the minor. Emancipation does not include the right to buy alcohol or cigarettes unless they are of legal age. A 17 year-old teen can join the military with parental consent. a minor's financial independence from their parents does not automatically meet the criteria for emancipation in most U.S. jurisdictions. While financial self-sufficiency is a core requirement in nearly all states, it is typically just one factor in a broader evaluation process that usually requires court approval. Emancipation is not granted solely based on finances; the minor must also demonstrate maturity, stable living arrangements, and the ability to manage their own affairs without parental support. Case Files in Family Medicine, Toy, Briscoe, Britton, Heidelbaugh, Fourth edition, page 95, 100",
      "exp_hant": "對於18歲以下的病患而言，「解放」是一種法律程序，未成年人可自行向法院請求宣告為法定成年人。解放相關法律因州而異。解放終止了父母對未成年人的扶養義務與法律責任，也終止了父母為未成年人做出醫療決定的權利。解放並不包含購買酒類或香菸的權利，除非其已達法定年齡。17歲的青少年在父母同意下可以從軍。未成年人在經濟上不依賴父母，在美國大多數司法管轄區並不會自動符合解放的條件。雖然經濟自立在幾乎所有州都是核心要求之一，但通常只是整體評估中的一項因素，且通常仍需法院核准。解放並非僅憑經濟狀況即可核准；未成年人還須證明其成熟度、穩定的生活安排，以及在無父母支持下自行處理事務的能力。Case Files in Family Medicine, Toy, Briscoe, Britton, Heidelbaugh, Fourth edition, page 95, 100"
