confirmation prior to sending the message. Further, while the Privacy Rule does not prohibit the use of unencrypted e-mail for treatment-related communications between health care providers and patients, other safeguards should be applied to reasonably protect privacy, such as limiting the amount or type of information disclosed through the unencrypted e-mail. In addition, covered entities will want to ensure that any transmission of electronic protected health information is in compliance with the HIPAA Security Rule requirements at 45 C.F.R. Part 164, Subpart C. A patient has the right to request changes to their medical records. However, the medical provider is not obligated to correct those errors if the provider makes a professional judgment that the records need not be amended. If the provider refuses to make changes, the patient may write a dispute letter about the errors which is to be included in the file of the patient. www.hhs.gov/hipaa/",
