    {
      "number": 128,
      "question_en": "A 55 year-old female has chronic edema for the main complaint. She often feels cold, has cold hands and feet, low back pain, feels weak, poor appetite, and loose stools. The acupuncture prescription is BL-20 (pi shu), BL-23 (shen shu), REN-9 (shuifen), KI-7 (fu liu), REN-4 (guan yuan), and ST-36 (zu san li). After the first treatment she returns complaining of facial puffiness. How would you modify the treatment?",
      "question_hant": "一名55歲女性以慢性水腫為主訴。她常感畏冷、手腳冰冷、腰痛、體虛、食慾不振及大便溏瀉。針灸處方為脾俞（BL-20）、腎俞（BL-23）、水分（REN-9）、復溜（KI-7）、關元（REN-4）及足三里（ST-36）。第一次治療後，她回診並主訴面部浮腫。你會如何調整治療？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "Add LU-7 (lie que)",
          "hant": "加入列缺",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "Add SP-5 (shang qiu)",
          "hant": "加入商丘",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "Add DU-26 (ren zhong)",
          "hant": "加入人中",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Add LI-6 (pian li)",
          "hant": "加入偏歷",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Edema is of the yin type (Kidney and Spleen Deficiency) which is chronic while the yang type (external wind) is acute. Yin edema is the result of the weak yang of the Kidneys and Spleen failing to control the Water Passages resulting in the accumulation of Body Fluids. Needling and moxa on BL-20, BL-23, and KI-7 warms the yang of the Kidneys and Spleen to remove the accumulated Dampness. Treating REN-4 and REN-9 promotes the circulation of Body Fluids and strengthens the primary qi. ST-36 promotes the transformation and transportation of water and strengthens the overall body qi. DU-26 is the meeting point with the Large Intestine channel with the Du Mai channel, both of which dominate the face and nose. DU-26 regulates the Water Passages of the Upper Jiao and treats puffiness of the face. SP-5 (as well as GB-41) is an effective point for treating edema of the foot and ankle. LI-6 is selected when external Wind obstructs the Water Passages resulting in edema. Similarly, LU-7 is selected when external Wind interferes with the Lung function of regulating the Water Passages and descending the Body Fluids to the Bladder. CAM, 3rd Edition, page 431; CAM, 4th Edition, page 527",
      "exp_hant": "此水腫屬陰水型（腎脾兩虛），病程屬慢性；而陽水型（外感風邪）則屬急性。陰水是因腎脾陽氣虛弱，無法約束水道，導致津液積聚所致。針灸並灸脾俞、腎俞及復溜，能溫補腎脾之陽以袪除積聚之濕邪。治療關元與水分能促進津液運行並增強元氣。足三里能促進水液的氣化與運行，並增強整體正氣。人中是大腸經與督脈的交會穴，此二經皆主司面部與鼻部，人中能調理上焦水道，並治療面部浮腫。商丘（連同足臨泣）是治療足踝部水腫的有效穴位。偏歷用於外感風邪阻滯水道所致之水腫。同樣地，列缺用於外感風邪干擾肺之調節水道及使津液下輸膀胱之功能時。CAM, 3rd Edition, page 431; CAM, 4th Edition, page 527"
    },
    {
      "number": 129,
      "question_en": "Which pulse is described as floating, broad and comes with strength and recedes with weakness?",
      "question_hant": "下列何種脈象的特徵為浮而大，來時有力，去時無力？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "soggy pulse (ru mai)",
          "hant": "濡脈",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "slippery pulse (hua mai)",
          "hant": "滑脈",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "flooding pulse (hong mai)",
          "hant": "洪脈",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "moving pulse (dong mai)",
          "hant": "動脈",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "A flooding pulse (hong mai) surges with the strength of a big pulse (da mai) that hits the palpatory fingers on the superficial, middle, and deep levels. However, when the flooding pulse (hong mai) recedes from the fingers, it becomes weak and loses some of its strength. A flooding pulse (hong mai) is like a wave that hits strong and recedes with weakness. A flooding pulse (hong mai) can indicate that Heat has injured the Body Fluids and Yin. A soggy pulse (ru mai) is a combination of a thin pulse (xi mai), an empty pulse (xu mai), and a floating pulse (fu mai). A soggy pulse (ru mai) indicates Deficient Blood, Deficient Jing or Dampness. A moving pulse (dong mai) combines the short pulse, the tight pulse, the slippery pulse, and the rapid pulse. It is an incomplete pulse as it is only felt in one position like a bean and not having a head or tail. It indicates an extreme condition (fever, pain, fright, palpitations) and is rarely encountered in practice. A slippery pulse (hua mai) is categorized according to shape. A slippery pulse (hua mai) feels very smooth and wavy like a \"slithering snake\" or like pearls rolling in a dish. It indicates Excess, Dampness, Phlegm, or pregnancy. The Web That Has No Weaver, Kaptchuk, 2000, page 204",
      "exp_hant": "洪脈以大脈之力來勢洶湧，於浮、中、沉三部皆能觸及。然而，當洪脈退去時，則轉為無力並失去部分力度。洪脈猶如波浪，來勢強勁，去時卻轉弱。洪脈可顯示熱邪已損傷津液與陰分。濡脈是細脈、虛脈與浮脈三者的結合，顯示血虛、精虧或濕邪。動脈結合了短脈、緊脈、滑脈及數脈的特徵，屬於一種不完整的脈象，僅能在某一部位觸及，形如豆粒，無頭無尾，顯示極端病況（發熱、疼痛、驚恐、心悸），臨床上極為罕見。滑脈依形態分類，觸感非常圓滑流暢，如「游走之蛇」，又如珠走玉盤，顯示實證、濕證、痰證或懷孕。The Web That Has No Weaver, Kaptchuk, 2000, page 204"
    },
