      "exp_en": "Shu mai (rapid pulse) indicates a hot pattern, full-heat (rapid-full), or empty-heat (rapid-floating). The pulse has five beats or more per each respiration cyle of the practioner. In modern times a watch is used. Rough normal rates per minute: age 1-4 (greater than 91 beats), age 4-10 (greater than 85 beats), age 10-16 (greater than 81 beats), age 16-35 (greater than 77 beats), age 35-50 (greater than 71 beats), 50+ (greater than 69 beats). CAM writes that a shu mai is five beats per breath or more than 90 beats per minute. It indicates the presence of heat which accelerates the blood circulation. If there is internal excess heat, the antipathogenic qi is still strong and struggling with the pathogenic factor and this will produce a rapid and forceful pulse. Empty fire resulting from deficient yin produces a rapid and weak pulse. Yang can be so deficient that it can float resulting in a rapid, large, and weak pulse. Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 384; CAM, 3rd Edition, page 290",
      "exp_hant": "數脈主熱證，可為實熱（數而有力）或虛熱（數而浮）。以醫者一次呼吸為單位，脈搏跳動五次以上即為數脈；現代則多以手錶計時。各年齡層的大致正常脈率為：1至4歲（大於91次／分）、4至10歲（大於85次／分）、10至16歲（大於81次／分）、16至35歲（大於77次／分）、35至50歲（大於71次／分）、50歲以上（大於69次／分）。CAM一書指出，數脈為一呼一吸間跳動五次以上，或每分鐘超過90次。數脈顯示熱邪加速血液循環。若體內有實熱，正氣仍盛而與邪氣相爭，會產生數而有力之脈；陰虛所致的虛火，則產生數而無力之脈。陽氣極度虛衰時可浮越於外，形成數、大而無力之脈。Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 384; CAM, 3rd Edition, page 290"
