      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. SCATTERED PULSE is an empty type pulse. Rather than the pulse flowing, the scattered pulse feels like it is broken into tiny dots. The pulse is relatively superficial and disappears with stronger palpation. The scattered pulse indicates severe qi and Blood xu, especially xu of Kidney qi and Original Qi. This pulse indicates a serious disorder. If this pulse manifests in pregnancy, it can indicate a miscarriage can occur. The FIRM PULSE is an excess type of pulse. It is palpated only at the deep level, feels hard, full, wiry, and long. It is a wiry pulse only at the middle and deep levels whereas a true wiry pulse is palpated at all three levels. The firm pulse indicates inner Cold that can be associated with old pain. It can also mean inner accumulation of stuck qi or Blood. The CHOPPY PULSE is one of the eight basic pulses. This pulse feels rough, jagged, and is short. It does not have a wave like a normal pulse does which flows from the rear to the front in a smooth and continuous wave. The choppy pulse does not have this continuous wave movement through the three positions. The choppy pulse is felt in each of the three positions separately. The choppy pulse indicates xu of Blood or Essence. It can also manifest after profuse sweating, vomiting, or diarrhea. It is a bad sign to have this pulse during pregnancy and can indicate a miscarriage is about to happen. The WIRY PULSE is a type of full pulse. It feels superficial and hard and can be clearly palpated at the deep and middle levels. The pulse hits the fingers with force and feels like a guitar string so that when you try to push it down it wants to spring up. The wiry pulse indicates full type of Liver disorders such a stuck Liver qi or Blood, Liver Yang Rising, Liver Fire, Liver Wind. It can also indicate Phlegm or chronic pain. It is often felt in elderly patients. Diagnosis in Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2004, page 497; in his Foundations book he does not list the wiry pulse for blood stasis but only for Liver qi stasis, pain, and phlegm",
      "exp_hant": "此為最完整且正確之答案。散脈屬虛證之脈。散脈並非流暢連貫,而是感覺如散碎成細小點狀。此脈較為浮淺,重按則消失。散脈顯示嚴重氣血兩虛,尤其是腎氣及元氣之虛。此脈顯示病情嚴重。若於妊娠期間出現此脈,可能顯示將發生流產。牢脈屬實證之脈。僅於沉取層次可觸及,感覺堅硬、有力、弦而長。牢脈僅於中、沉二部呈弦象,而真正之弦脈則於浮、中、沉三部皆可觸及。牢脈顯示內寒,並可能與宿疾舊痛有關,亦可能表示體內氣血鬱結積聚。澀脈為八種基本脈象之一。此脈感覺粗糙、參差不齊且短促。它不像正常脈象般具有由後(尺)至前(寸)平順連續之波動感。澀脈缺乏此種連續性的波動運行,而是於寸、關、尺三部分別各自觸及。澀脈顯示血虛或精虛,亦可見於大汗、嘔吐或腹瀉之後。妊娠期間出現此脈為不良徵兆,可能顯示即將流產。弦脈屬實脈之一種。此脈觸感浮淺而堅硬,於中、沉二部皆可清楚觸及。此脈搏動有力,如按琴弦,下按時有回彈之感。弦脈顯示肝之實證,如肝氣鬱結或血瘀、肝陽上亢、肝火、肝風。亦可顯示痰邪或慢性疼痛。年長患者常見此脈。Diagnosis in Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2004, page 497;但在其《Foundations of Chinese Medicine》一書中,並未將弦脈列為血瘀之脈象,而僅列為肝氣鬱結、疼痛及痰證之脈。"
