          "hant": "舌象",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "In cases that have a mixed deficiency/excess condition, the strength of the pulse assists the practitioner in deciding the relative importance of the excess or the deficiency. In a mixed deficiency/excess condition, if the deficiency is primary the pulse will be weak or empty. If the excess condition is primary, then the pulse will be of an excess type of pulse. For example, a patient can have Liver Qi Stasis (excess) with Spleen Qi Deficiency. If the pulse is weak or empty, then the Spleen Qi Deficiency is primary. The treatment principle would be to tonify the upright qi. On the other hand, if the pulse is wiry and full, then the Liver Qi Stasis is primary. The treatment principle then becomes one of moving the Liver qi. Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 1181; 2005 Edition, page 1124",
      "exp_hant": "在虛實夾雜之病例中,脈象之強弱有助於臨床醫師判斷虛證或實證何者相對較為重要。在虛實夾雜之病證中,若虛為主,則脈象偏弱或虛;若實為主,則脈象呈實脈之類型。舉例而言,患者可能同時有肝氣鬱結(實)與脾氣虛(虛)。若脈弱或虛,則脾氣虛為主,治療原則應以扶正(補氣)為主。反之,若脈弦而實,則肝氣鬱結為主,治療原則則應以疏肝氣為主。Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 1181; 2005 Edition, page 1124"
