    {
      "number": 485,
      "question_en": "The Chinese term \"Shen\" can refer to",
      "question_hant": "中文「神」一詞可用以指稱下列何者？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "the complex of Jing, Qi, and Mind",
          "hant": "精、氣與心神之綜合體",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "the Mind or all five spiritual aspects",
          "hant": "心神，或五神（五種精神層面）",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "the mind",
          "hant": "心神",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "all five mental-spiritual aspects",
          "hant": "全部五種精神層面",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Shen is most often translated as Spirit. Shen can have many meanings. Shen can refer to the Mind that is associated with the Heart. In this view, Shen is the activity of thinking, consciousness, self-identity, insight, and memory (the Heart-Mind). The Heart is considered to be the residence of the Mind. Another way is to view Shen as Spirit as referring to all the five mental-spiritual aspects (Mind Heart, Ethereal Soul-Liver, Corporal Soul-Lungs, Intellect-Spleen, Will Power-Kidneys). The book \"Principles of Medical Practice\" writes \"The Mind (Shen) of the Heart gathers and unites with the Hun and the Po and it combines the Yi and the Zhi.\" These five mental-spiritual aspects are sometimes called the \"Five-Shens\". Another meaning of Shen is in relation to diagnosis. From this view, Shen can be observed in the facial complexion, eyes, tongue, and pulse, and demeanor. The Practice of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2008, page 243-244, 248",
      "exp_hant": "「神」最常被翻譯為「精神」（Spirit）。「神」一詞可有多重涵義。其一，「神」可指與心相關之心神，此觀點下，「神」即為思考、意識、自我認同、洞察力與記憶等活動（即心神）。心被視為神所居之處。另一種觀點則將「神」視為統攝五種精神層面之總稱（心之神、肝之魂、肺之魄、脾之意、腎之志）。《醫學正傳》一書中寫道：「心之神統攝並結合魂與魄，並統合意與志。」此五種精神層面有時亦被稱為「五神」。「神」的另一層涵義則與診斷相關：從此角度而言，可從面色、眼神、舌象、脈象及神情舉止觀察「神」的狀態。《中醫臨床學》，Maciocia，2008年，第243-244、248頁"
    },
    {
      "number": 486,
      "question_en": "The coming and going of the Hun has its counterpart in the",
      "question_hant": "魂之往來（出入）與下列何者相對應？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "free flow of Liver qi",
          "hant": "肝氣之疏泄",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "Zhi (Will-power) of the Kidneys",
          "hant": "腎之志",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "bile of the Gall Bladder",
          "hant": "膽之膽汁",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Po (Corporal Soul) of the Lungs",
          "hant": "肺之魄",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "The coming and going of the Hun has its counterpart in the free flow of Liver qi. The coming and going of the Hun refers to the intuition, inspiration, creativity, dreaming, life-dreams and goals/projects, imagination, and movement toward others. The coming and going of the Hun needs to be regulated and balanced just like in the free flow of Liver qi. If the coming and going of the Hun is deficient (not enough coming and going) there is depression just as in Liver qi stasis. If the coming and going of the Hun is in excess (too much coming and going) the person becomes agitated, too emotional, or manic just as can occur with Liver Fire or Hyperactive Liver yang. The Hun helps to maintain a balance between excitation and restraint under the direction of the Heart and Mind. BL-47 (hun men) and LR-3 (tai chong) are points that balance the coming and going of the Hun. The Practice of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2008, page 255",
      "exp_hant": "魂之往來（出入）與肝氣之疏泄相對應。魂之往來是指直覺、靈感、創造力、做夢、人生夢想與目標／計畫、想像力，以及與他人交往互動之能力。魂之往來需如肝氣疏泄一般，維持調節與平衡。若魂之往來不足（出入不夠），則會如肝氣鬱滯一般而出現情緒低落；若魂之往來太過（出入過度），則會如肝火或肝陽亢盛一般而出現躁動、情緒過度激動或躁狂。魂在心神的主導下，協助維持興奮與抑制之間的平衡。魂門（BL-47）與太衝（LR-3）為能調節魂之往來的穴位。《中醫臨床學》，Maciocia，2008年，第255頁"
    },
