          "hant": "每季的前18天",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "Early Summer, Early Spring, Early Autumn, Early Winter",
          "hant": "初夏、初春、初秋、初冬",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Late Summer",
          "hant": "長夏",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "The ancient Chinese texts did not assign the Earth element to any particular season as the other elements were. Earth does not correspond to a season because Earth is seen as the stable center or pivot around which the other seasons/elements spin. The text \"Classic of Categories\" (1624 AD) writes that \"The Spleen belongs to Earth which belongs to the Center, its influence manifests for 18 days at the end of each of the four seasons and it does not belong to any season on its own\". From this perspective, the Earth element corresponds to the late stage of each season. That is, during the last 18 days of each season the heavenly energies return to the Earth for replenishment. Earth is most often associated with Late Summer but it is also associated with Late Spring, Late Autumn, and Late Winter. The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 23; 2005 Edition, page 23; 1989 Edition, page 18",
      "exp_hant": "古代中國典籍並未如其他行一般，將土行分派給某一特定的季節。土之所以不對應任何單一季節，是因為土被視為穩定的中心或樞紐，其他季節（行）皆圍繞其運轉。《類經》（成書於西元1624年）記載：「脾屬土，屬中央，其氣旺於四季之末各十八日，本身不獨主一季」。由此觀點，土行對應的是每一季的末期，亦即每季最後十八天，天之氣回歸於地以獲得補充。土最常與長夏相對應，但亦與季春、季秋、季冬相關。The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 23; 2005 Edition, page 23; 1989 Edition, page 18"
    },
