      "exp_en": "Antimuscarinic agents treat GI spasms (irritable bowel syndrome), tremors due to Parkinson's or effect of other drugs, coughing, nausea and vomiting. From a TCM perspective, Antimuscarinic Agents are posited to be qi-rectifiers whose nature is acrid and windy and tend to disperse the qi and plunder the yin. The side effects of anti-muscarinic agents include blurred vision, confusion, constipation, drowsiness, dry mouth, agitation, headache, rapid pulse, and urinary retention. Anti-muscarinic agents can dry up body fluids (constipation) and can cause yin vacuity (dry mouth from Stomach yin vacuity. Urinary retention from Kidney yin vacuity, agitation rapid pulse from vacuity Heat, headache from hyperactive yang rooted in yin vacuity). If anti-muscarinic agents cause too much of a qi dispersal it can result in qi vacuity symptoms (drowsiness, fatigue). Integrated Pharmacology, Sperber and Flaws, 2007, page 348-349",
      "exp_hant": "抗毒蕈鹼藥物用於治療腸胃痙攣（腸躁症）、帕金森氏病或其他藥物所致的震顫、咳嗽、噁心與嘔吐。從中醫角度來看，抗毒蕈鹼藥物被視為理氣藥，其性辛而具風性，容易耗散氣機並劫奪陰液。抗毒蕈鹼藥物的副作用包括視物模糊、神志不清、便秘、嗜睡、口乾、煩躁、頭痛、脈數，以及尿瀦留。抗毒蕈鹼藥物可耗傷津液（便秘）並導致陰虛（口乾為胃陰虛所致；尿瀦留為腎陰虛所致；煩躁脈數為虛熱所致；頭痛為陰虛陽亢所致）。若抗毒蕈鹼藥物耗散氣機過度，則可能出現氣虛症狀（嗜睡、疲乏）。Integrated Pharmacology, Sperber and Flaws, 2007, page 348-349"
    },
    {
      "number": 562,
