      "exp_en": "The ancient texts considered the left Kidney as the kidney proper related to the Bladder and the right Kidney as the ming men related to the triple burner. Later on, during the Ming dynasty, the ming men was positioned as between the two kidneys. The ming men was viewed as the organ of Water and Fire, the residence of yin and yang, the Sea of Essence, and the determiner of life and death. The ming men Fire is understood to be the motive force of all physiological processes. It is the \"pilot light\" which ignites all of the other body processes. As such, the ming men is viewed as the \"True Fire\" or \"Minister Fire\". The ming men as Minister Fire is in a different sense than that of Pericardium as the Minister Fire in Five Element theory in which Fire comes from the Heart and not from the ming men (Kidneys). These two theories of \"Minister Fire\" are both valid and have grown from two different perspectives. The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 162; 2005 Edition, page 160; 1989 Edition, page 98",
      "exp_hant": "古代典籍認為左腎為與膀胱相關的真正之腎，右腎則為與三焦相關的命門。其後至明代，命門被定位於兩腎之間。命門被視為水火之臟、陰陽之宅、精之海，以及生死之所繫。命門之火被理解為一切生理過程的動力，是點燃其他一切身體機能的「導火苗」。因此，命門被視為「真火」或「相火」。命門作為相火，與五行理論中心包作為相火（此火源於心而非命門/腎）的意義有所不同。這兩種「相火」理論皆屬有效，只是源自不同的角度。The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 162; 2005 Edition, page 160; 1989 Edition, page 98"
