      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. Circulation of yin and yang does not indicate an imbalance. Yin and yang mutually consume each other as they are in a constant state of dynamic balance that continually recalibrate their relative levels. When yin is weak, then yang appears as a relative excess and vice versa. In this imbalance, the proportion of yin and yang recalibrate until a new balance is achieved. When yin is preponderant it creates a decrease of yang (yin consumes yang) and vice versa (yang consumes yin). If preponderance of yin is primary there will be an excess of yin which will then consume the yang leading to an actual weak yang. If weakness of yang is primary, it will lead to an apparent excess of yin relative to yang xu. The same principle is valid for preponderance of yang and weakness of yin. The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 7; 2005 Edition, page 7; 1989 Edition, page 6",
