        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. A tight pulse (jin mai) is a strong pulse that feels like it bounces from side to side like a tight rope. A tight pulse (jin mai) is fuller and more elastic than a wiry pulse (xuan mai). A tight pulse (jin mai) feels like it vibrates and seems to be faster than it actually is. A tight pulse (jin mai) indicates Excess, Cold, or Stagnation. An empty pulse (xu mai) is categorized according to strength (it is weak). It is big, weak, superficial, and soft like a partially filled water balloon. It indicates Deficient Qi and Blood. A thin pulse is categorized according to shape (thread like) but distinct and clear. It indicates Deficient Blood and Qi failing to fill the pulse. A short pulse (duan mai) is categorized according to length. This pulse does not fill all the horizontal positions under the fingers and felt, usually, only in one position under the fingers. It indicates Deficient Qi. The Web That Has No Weaver, Kaptchuk, 2000, page 200",
      "exp_hant": "這是最完整且正確的答案。緊脈是有力的脈象，感覺如緊繃的繩索般左右彈動。緊脈比弦脈更飽滿、更具彈性。緊脈給人一種震動的感覺，速率看似比實際更快。緊脈主實證、寒證或氣血阻滯。虛脈是依脈力分類（脈力弱），脈大、無力、浮而軟，如半充氣的水球，主氣血兩虛。細脈是依脈形分類（如線狀），但脈形清晰分明，主血虛及氣不能充盈脈道。短脈是依脈長分類，此脈無法充滿指下所有橫向部位，通常僅在單一部位觸及，主氣虛。The Web That Has No Weaver, Kaptchuk, 2000, page 200"
