      "exp_en": "The bitter taste, hypochondrial distension, blurry vision, irritability, nausea, vomiting, and wiry pulse all point to a Gall Bladder disharmony. When an external pathogenic factor enters the body, the Upright qi (zheng qi) resists the external pathogen. At the tai yang stage, this struggle manifests as an aversion to cold. When the external pathogen goes deeper and transforms into Heat, there is an aversion to heat. In the shao yang stage there is an alternation between tai yang (aversion to cold) and yang ming (aversion to heat). The aversion to cold and the aversion to heat do not occur simultaneously but alternate. Shao yang is a half-interior and half-exterior pattern because the pathogenic factor moves back and forth between tai yang and yang ming. Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 759; 2005 Edition, page 716",
      "exp_hant": "口苦、脅肋脹滿、視物模糊、煩躁、噁心、嘔吐及弦脈，皆指向膽的失調。當外邪侵入人體時，正氣會與外邪相抗爭。在太陽階段，此相爭表現為惡寒。當外邪深入並化熱時，則出現惡熱。少陽階段則在太陽（惡寒）與陽明（惡熱）之間交替出現。惡寒與惡熱並非同時發生，而是交替出現。少陽屬半表半裡證，因邪氣在太陽與陽明之間往返移動。Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 759; 2005 Edition, page 716"
